{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' collab/1790110150738-reviewer-hash-registry-plan.glm-5.3.stdout.log && sed -n '261,620p' collab/1790110150738-reviewer-hash-registry-plan.glm-5.3.stdout.log && sed -n '200,245p' src/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc && sed -n '160,205p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc && sed -n '265,305p' src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\nWe're changing auto mode to no longer charge for classifier requests in Claude Code. However, this session isn't eligible because your requests go through open.bigmodel.cn, which isn't compatible with this update. Nothing breaks: auto mode keeps working, and its classifier requests are billed as before. To fix it and access the new version of auto mode, ask your gateway to implement: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/auto-mode-classifier-billing\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-22 20:59:17 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-23 03:59:17 +07 (Indochina Time)\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: pending (caller-generated; not exposed to this session)\n\n**Verdict: READY WITH CHANGES.** No P1. Sol's call-site audit is accurate at every file:line I could check (all ~18 validation sites, the primitive-profile mislabel bug, the bare DaoSpace `:schema-hash`, the register implementation's absence from master), the encoding-id-in-address architecture is correct against `dao.jing.md`'s documented CBOR migration, and the H/R disposition matches both the code and the register design doc. Two P2 corrections should be folded in before the plan is promoted into `docs/design/`; the P3s are editorial. No rework needed — promote with changes.\n\n## Verification scorecard (facts checked against source, not assumed)\n\n1. **Direct `jing/sha256` consumers — all five confirmed, no sixth exists.** `debruijn_code.cljc:530` (`descriptor-hash`), `debruijn_code.cljc:546` (`image-hash`), `debruijn.cljc:952` (`dimension-hash`), `debruijn.cljc:1008` (`node-hash`), `dht.cljc:34` (`node-id`). Exhaustive by grepping `jing/sha256` across `src/cljc`.\n\n2. **primitive-profile mislabel — confirmed, and it is the only `sha256` string literal outside dao.jing.cljc.** `vm.cljc:136` reads `(keyword \"yin.k.pp\" (str \"sha256-\" (jing/content-hash description)))` — after a default flip this mints `:yin.k.pp/sha256-<blake3-digest>`. A real bug, correctly called, not a style nit.\n\n3. **Bare checkpoint `:schema-hash` — confirmed.** `dao/space/index.cljc:1294` persists `(jing/content-hash (:schema consumer))` with no algorithm tag; `:1370` re-verifies it against the ambient default on restore. Exactly the \"not algorithm-agile\" defect Sol describes.\n\n4. **The ~18 validation sites — every one confirmed**, with a mechanism split Sol's text glosses over: the dao.jing backends compare `segment-hash` vs `content-hash` (`jing.cljc:414` materialize! `:present` read-back; `mem.cljc:29`; `file.cljc:76`; `remote.cljc:41`; `dht.cljc:162`; `dht/node.cljc:114`; `remote/step.cljc:351-352`), while the yin.vm/DaoSpace consumers **re-mint with `segment-key` and compare whole addresses** (`content.cljc:101,137`; `macro.cljc:276`; `semantic.cljc:618-619`; `completion.cljc:292`; `ledger.cljc:187,195,203`; `vm.cljc:1248`; `space/index.cljc:315-316` read-manifest; `dht.cljc:224`). Same defect — default-following verification — and `segment-matches?` fixes both shapes, so Sol's substance is right even where his mechanism description is loose.\n\n5. **Register absence from master — confirmed.** No `debruijn_register_code.cljc` or contract test on master; both live on the `register-r0` branch (worktree at `0e497a44`, commits `483bbffc`/`0e497a44`). Refusing to plan edits against nonexistent files was correct — the orchestrator's own prompt wrongly asserted those files exist on this checkout; Sol caught it.\n\n6. **The core architectural claim (CBOR breaks algorithm-only addresses) — confirmed.** `dao.jing.md:176-197` (\"Canonical encoding\") declares the printer transitional, and the open item at `dao.jing.md:413-418` says verbatim that when the encoding lands, \"`content-hash`, `segment-key`, and every minted address change together.\" The breakage is concrete, not hypothetical: `dao.jing.file` re-validates every replayed frame against the *current* default at open (`file.cljc:73-79` through `replay-frames`/open), so a pre-migration store would fail to open at all, not merely re-hash. An algorithm-only address cannot distinguish print-minted from CBOR-minted preimages; verifying under \"both\" encodings would widen the accepted preimage set and break fail-closed parsing. Pinning bare `sha256-` to print-v1+SHA-256 by fiat and carrying an explicit encoding id on new addresses is the correct resolution.\n\n7. **H/R disposition — confirmed sound.** H is computed today by direct `jing/sha256` calls that never consult any default (`debruijn_code.cljc:530,546`; `debruijn.cljc:952,1008`), so \"freezing\" means keeping the code as-is plus the default-flip regression test. The register doc pins `R = sha256(register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)` (`yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:285`) with the contract version inside R, and register-r0 implements exactly that. Decoupling H/R from `default-hash-algorithm` matches both the code and the doc; the plan's format-identity vs stored-value-identity distinction is how `dao.jing.md` already frames storage ignorance.\n\n8. **Scope discipline — clean.** No phase rehashes or rewrites stored content; the checkpoint change is additive (new candidates carry `:schema-address`; legacy candidates interpreted explicitly as print-v1/SHA-256); H3 flips the hash default only, never the encoding default; the non-goals list matches what the phases actually do. The H-phase boxes match the house R-phase box style (`New/Existing edits/Must not change`).\n\n## Findings\n\n**P2-1 — Three copy-path sites are misclassified as `segment-matches?` validation sites; following the per-site instruction literally yields code that throws on all legacy content after H3.**\n- `dao.jing.dht` `make-get`: `dht.cljc:229` writes fetched content into the local store via one-argument `(jing/materialize! local value)` and compares to the fetched address; `:233` throws on mismatch. After the default flip, materializing a legacy SHA payload mints `print-v1+blake3-…` → every DHT fetch of pre-flip content throws \"fetched content cached under the wrong address.\"\n- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate!` sync `pull!`: `storage.cljc:186` — same shape; throws \"hydration address mismatch\" (`:188`) on every pre-flip blob.\n- The async sibling `fetched!`: `storage.cljc:286` / `:290` — same.\n\nFailure scenario: H3 ships, an operator hydrates a pre-flip index from a remote source; `hydrate!` fails on the root blob despite the content being intact. Fix: classify these as address-directed **mint** sites — they must call the new explicit-arity `materialize!` with `{:encoding (segment-encoding addr), :algorithm (segment-algorithm addr)}`. The mechanism already exists in the plan's `materialize!` section; only the site classification is wrong. H2's completion criteria (\"a fetched old SHA object verifies while the test temporarily binds a BLAKE3 minting default\"; \"B-tree hydration … accept and verify both\") would catch the bug at test time, so this is an instruction-correctness fix, not a silent-breakage risk.\n\n**P2-2 — The plan never mentions that a canonical CBOR codec already exists in the tree and is corpus-frozen.** `src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (J1/J2 landed, commits `7968884b`, `e3917e69`) implements canonical CBOR encode/decode behind the frozen corpus `test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json`; today it is used only for `encoded-compare` (`dao/space/query.cljc:850`), not for addressing. Two consequences:\n- H2/H4's \"a simulated second encoding profile proves that verification dispatches by the address\" — a real, already-frozen second encoder exists. Using `dao.jing.cbor/encode` as the test profile is strictly stronger evidence than a fabricated profile and invents nothing. If a simulated profile is still preferred (e.g. to keep the registry closed against a not-yet-ratified id), the plan should say so explicitly rather than leaving `dao.jing.cbor` unacknowledged.\n- H5 should state that the future `cbor-v1` profile is expected to be backed by this codec and corpus (the plan's `:segment/cbor-v1+…` example already matches the corpus name), and note the value-domain difference: the print profile throws on records, while CBOR has its own refusal classes (`::refusal` `:non-canonical`, `:unpaired-surrogate`, …) — a cbor-v1 profile will not cover every value print-v1 covers. One sentence in H5 closes it.\n\n**P3-1** — `file.cljc:82-97` `validate-codec-round-trip!` is listed under sites that \"must use `segment-matches?`\", but it is not an address/payload check: it compares `content-hash` of a payload against its own `pr-str` round-trip (both sides default-minted, no address involved). The fix is to compute both sides under the mint profile; `segment-matches?` does not apply. Wording fix.\n\n**P3-2** — No regression guard against a future call site reintroducing default-following verification. H0's classification is a one-time manual audit with no committed artifact and no enforcement. Commit the classification table in H0, and add a lint (clj-kondo rule or test) forbidding `jing/content-hash` / `jing/segment-key` in equality positions outside `dao.jing` — or at minimum make \"verification must be address-directed; minting primitives are not validators\" a stated law in `dao.jing.md` at H5.\n\n**P3-3** — H2's completion criteria name a subset of consumers (\"memory, file replay, remote, stepped remote, DHT, B-tree hydration, DaoSpace manifests, and yin.vm content loading\") while the site list also covers yin.vm.macro, yin.vm.semantic, yin.vm.completion, yin.vm.ledger, and yin.vm's row validation. The criteria should reference the full site list so the phase cannot be greened on the subset.\n\n**P3-4** — The \"portability spike\" cited as the source of the three provider choices left no artifact in the repo (no collab log, no doc; it exists only as assertions in the orchestrator's prompt). H0/H1's in-repo official-vector fixtures properly replace its evidence; consider committing the spike's cross-provider digest table as a fixture for provenance.\n\n**P3-5** — `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` (`debruijn.cljc:946-952`) is a `def` (a value computed at load), not a `defn` — the H0 audit table should describe it as such, since \"call site\" is inexact for it.\n\n**P3-6** — Grammar parse rule deserves one explicit sentence: encoding ids contain `-` (`print-v1`, future `cbor-v1`), so the parser cannot split on `-` by character class; the plan's \"match registered address identifiers\" implies registry-membership matching — say so, to prevent an implementer writing a regex split.\n\n## Disposition\n\nPromote into `docs/design/` **with changes**: fold in P2-1 (reclassify the three copy-path sites as explicit-arity `materialize!` mint sites) and P2-2 (acknowledge `dao.jing.cbor`, decide real-vs-simulated second profile for the H2/H4 dispatch test, add the cbor-v1 backing/value-domain sentence to H5). The P3s land as editorial fixes during promotion. The architecture itself — dual-id addresses, closed registries, address-directed verification, H/R frozen independently of the DaoJing default, hash-then-CBOR sequencing — survives adversarial reading and matches the actual code at every claim I could check.\n\nAll citations were resolved to file:line in the working tree at commit `bdfb62fa` (master) on 2026-09-23 via direct reads and greps of `dao/jing.cljc`, `dao/jing/{mem,file,remote,remote/step,dht,dht/node}.cljc`, `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc`, `dao/space/index.cljc`, the yin.vm namespaces, `docs/design/{dao.jing,datom.world,yin.vm.debruijn.register}.md`, `dao/jing/cbor.cljc`, and `git worktree list` / `git ls-tree register-r0`.\n                      (mapv (fn [peer]\n                              (future\n                                (safe-store-content! net peer address payload)))\n                            peers))\n           :default (run! (fn [peer]\n                            (safe-store-content! net peer address payload))\n                          peers)))\n      result)))\n\n\n(defn- make-get\n  [{:keys [net local], :as handle}]\n  (fn [address not-found]\n    (ensure-open handle)\n    (let [v (jing/get local address content-missing)]\n      (if (not (identical? v content-missing))\n        v\n        (let [self-id (:id (self-peer net))\n              fetched\n              (some (fn [peer]\n                      (when (not= self-id (:id peer))\n                        (when-let [res (safe-fetch-content net peer address)]\n                          (when (and (:found? res)\n                                     (= address\n                                        (jing/segment-key (:value res))))\n                            [(:value res)]))))\n                    (lookup net (content-target address)))]\n          (if fetched\n            (let [value (first fetched)\n                  cached (jing/materialize! local value)]\n              (when-not (= address cached)\n                (throw\n                  (ex-info\n                    \"dao.jing.dht: fetched content cached under the wrong address\"\n                    {:address address, :cached cached})))\n              value)\n            not-found))))))\n\n\n(defn- make-close\n  [{:keys [net local closed-atom]}]\n  (fn []\n    (with-lock closed-atom\n      (fn []\n        (when-not @closed-atom\n          (let [err-net (try (close-net! net)\n                                    (atom {:writing? false, :unacked []}))]\n     (vreset! box storage)\n     storage)))\n\n\n(defn hydrate!\n  \"Full-graph hydration (§5.4 rule 2, blocking variant): copy every blob\n   reachable from the set's root address out of the source backend into\n   the hydration cache, so subsequent reads — and mutations, whose write\n   path requires residency — succeed. Idempotent: already-cached segments\n   are re-materialized as no-ops. Returns the set. On a non-hydration\n   storage this is a no-op (sync backends need no hydration).\"\n  [s]\n  (let [storage (bt/set-storage s)]\n    (when (instance? HydrationStorage storage)\n      (let [^HydrationStorage hs storage\n            source (.-source hs)\n            cache (.-cache hs)]\n        (when (async-source? source)\n          (throw (ex-info \"hydrate! against an async backend; use hydrate-async\"\n                          {})))\n        (letfn [(pull!\n                  [addr]\n                  (let [blob (jing/get source addr absent)]\n                    (when (identical? blob absent)\n                      (throw (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address addr})))\n                    (let [addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob)]\n                      (when-not (= addr addr')\n                        (throw (ex-info \"hydration address mismatch\"\n                                        {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))\n                    (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (pull! a))))]\n          (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (pull! addr)))))\n    s))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Async variants (§5.4 rule 2 non-blocking, and durability ordering)\n\n(defn- deferred\n  \"The host's one-shot async value: a Promise (cljs), a Completer-backed\n   Future (cljd), a CompletableFuture (JVM). `start` receives resolve and\n   reject fns and calls exactly one of them.\"\n  [start]\n  #?(:cljd (let [c (async/Completer)]\n             (start #(.complete c %) #(.completeError c %))\n             (.-future c))\n                 (when-not (contains? (first (swap-vals! seen conj addr)) addr)\n                   (swap! outstanding inc)\n                   (let [blob (jing/get cache addr absent)]\n                     (if (identical? blob absent)\n                       (get-async addr #(report (attempt (fn [] (fetched! addr %)))\n                                                (fn [_] nil)\n                                                fail!))\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\n                           (done!))))))\n               (fetched!\n                 [addr c]\n                 (cond\n                   (not (contains? c :found?))\n                   (fail! (ex-info \"hydration fetch failed\"\n                                   {:address addr, :completion c}))\n\n                   (not (:found? c))\n                   (fail! (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address addr}))\n\n                   :else\n                   (let [blob (:value c)\n                         addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob)]\n                     (if (= addr addr')\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\n                           (done!))\n                       (fail! (ex-info \"hydration address mismatch\"\n                                       {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))))]\n         (report (attempt (fn []\n                            (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (visit! addr))\n                            (done!)))\n                 (fn [_] nil)\n                 fail!))\n       nil)\n     (report (attempt #(hydrate! s)) on-ok on-err))))\n\n\n(defn store-tree-async\n  \"The write-side sibling of hydrate-async (§5.4 durability ordering):\n   store the set's dirty subgraph into the hydration cache, push every\n   unacknowledged segment to the async source, and resolve to the root\n   address only after every one of those writes has acknowledged. The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
